
# Enterprise Security Header Checks
# These rules help prevent malicious emails and enforce security policies

# Block emails with dangerous attachments in Content-Type headers
/^Content-Type:.*application\/x-msdownload/    REJECT Executable attachments not allowed
/^Content-Type:.*application\/x-msdos-program/ REJECT Executable attachments not allowed  
/^Content-Type:.*application\/x-winexe/        REJECT Executable attachments not allowed
/^Content-Type:.*application\/x-ms-dos-executable/ REJECT Executable attachments not allowed

# Block suspicious filename extensions in Content-Disposition
/^Content-Disposition:.*filename=.*\.(exe|scr|bat|cmd|com|pif|vbs|js)"/i REJECT Dangerous attachment type blocked

# Block emails claiming to be from system accounts
/^From:.*\b(postmaster|mailer-daemon|apache|www-data|root|admin)@/i WARN Suspicious sender detected

# Rate limit based on suspicious patterns
/^Subject:.*\$\$\$/                          HOLD Possible spam - multiple dollar signs
/^Subject:.*(URGENT|WINNER|LOTTERY|MILLION)/i HOLD Possible spam - suspicious keywords

# Block emails with excessive HTML redirects
/^Content-Type:.*text\/html.*/ if /meta.*refresh.*url=/i REJECT Suspicious HTML redirect

# Monitor for potential phishing attempts
/^Subject:.*(verify.*account|update.*payment|suspended.*account)/i WARN Potential phishing attempt

# Block emails with suspicious Received headers indicating open relays
/^Received:.*\[(192\.168\.|10\.|172\.1[6-9]\.|172\.2[0-9]\.|172\.3[0-1]\.)/  WARN Internal IP in public route

# Security: Block emails with null bytes in headers
/\x00/ REJECT Null bytes in headers not allowed

# Configurable security rules (can be enabled/disabled via environment variables)
# These are logged but not rejected by default to minimize deliverability impact

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Subject capture for the email log (mailer/log_ingestor)
#
# Postfix's log records the envelope only -- sender, recipient, size, relay,
# status -- so the ingestor that builds mail_logs has no way to learn a
# message's subject, and every Email Logs row rendered with a blank subject.
# A WARN action makes cleanup echo the header into the log, which the ingestor
# then correlates back to the queue id.
#
# MUST STAY LAST. regexp header_checks stop at the first matching pattern, so
# a /^Subject:/ rule placed above the HOLD/WARN rules further up would
# short-circuit them and silently disable the spam-keyword and phishing checks.
# Ordered here, those still win and this only catches what they did not -- and
# because WARN logs the header either way, their lines are parsed for the
# subject too.
#
# WARN never rejects or defers, so this cannot affect deliverability. It does
# put subjects in plaintext in /var/log/postfix/mail.log; that is the tradeoff
# for showing them in the dashboard, and the log is root-readable only.
/^Subject:/ WARN subject-capture
